  "publication_text": {
    "summary": "The full ring of integers of Q(sqrt(10)) gives a complete elementary negative answer to the equality printed in Long Question 7.2 (arXiv:1512.04620v1), using its prime-element convention and without a PID assumption. Both the unit-difference and unit-or-prime-element-difference graphs have clique number two, and the least norm of a nonzero prime ideal is two. The displayed proposed equality therefore compares two with four.",
    "priority_statement": "This is a credited elementary exposition, with no claim of novelty, firstness, current worldwide openness, or independent discovery lineage. Lenstra's 1977 Proposition 2.2 already supplies the ideal-residue unit-clique bound. Dummit's 2021 notes explicitly contain the same classical ideal and norm obstruction. Long and Thistlethwaite supply the prime-clique framework; Lopez's 2018 thesis defines the same first prime graph and related higher prime-ideal-power graphs. Combining old ingredients here is not backdated as an earlier printed resolution. The exact historical priority of the full graph conclusion remains unestablished after the scoped audit.",
    "verification_scope": "The prime-element convention means a nonzero nonunit generating a prime ideal. It does not mean irreducible element and does not impose the additional Lenstra-Hurwitz unit-surjectivity condition. Higher prime-ideal-power graphs are different for powers at least two. The note computes the quantities in the printed question directly, independently of the journal theorem whose displayed minimum is over principal ideals. AI tools were used extensively in solving, reconstruction, source review, verification, drafting, and independent internal AI adversarial review. This work is unrefereed; no external human peer review, formal proof-assistant certification, or journal acceptance is claimed. Finite checks are diagnostics rather than a substitute for the written proof. The original ten files are preserved as the historical 11 October submission, including earlier access limitations; the current paper and adjudications control 17 October attribution and current source-reading claims. Three old edited input bodies are unavailable, so their transformation is not replay-certified. Candidate proof independence holds through proof/verdict completion, with late root status messages before its final seal explicitly disclosed. All proof families use the same parity/norm obstruction. The public query ledger omits provider excerpts; its initial local original is not portably authenticated. Sealed relative-scratch entrypoint is corrected by the separately retained reconstruction_replay.py; repository-dependent source authentication scripts require their original prerequisites and are not automatic portable checks."
  },
  "tracker": {
    "original_problem": "10900050 (AMR-108-0050): Long Question 7.2, prime-difference clique equality; https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/1268",
    "notes": "Complete negative answer to printed equality: Q(sqrt(10)), both clique numbers 2, minimum prime-ideal norm 2, proposed RHS4. Prime-element/full-integer-ring/no-PID scope. Classical Lenstra unit bound and Dummit arithmetic credited; full-result priority unestablished, no firstness/current-openness claim. Extensive AI use; unrefereed. Preprint and scoped verification archive."
  },
